Bard's right, weapon skills do differentiate player classes--if my
ranger can do a lot better with missile attacks, mixing in a spell here
or there, while my wizard gets by raining down magic missile on the
beasties, that makes playing the game with both classes more
interesting.

The problem is that they don't differentiate enough, and so a player
can wander around unskilled with Grayswandir and it's still better than
being skilled with snickersnee.  Because of that deficiency, gods
unlocking basic skill doesn't dramatically affect weapon choice.

Increasing the differentiation, at least to a degree, seems like a good
thing.  Perhaps make characters who are Unskilled have no level bonuses
to their to-hit with a weapon, and forbid the gods unrestricting
skills.  And make "skilled" level be necessary to get full Str/Dex
bonuses to-hit and damage.

Then all of a sudden playing a priest who is a partial spell caster who
uses a mace in melee and a sling/hammers/whatever as missiles makes a
lot of sense, instead of a higher level priest seeking the same weapons
as everyone else who can't twoweapon.

Essentially, I see unrestricting skill as a deficiency--but another
deficiency (the near-uselessness of weapon skills) masks it for now.



